          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Motivic cohomology is a cohomology theory for schemes which in many ways plays the rôle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cohomology
            in algebraic geometry. It was first conjectured to exist by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Beilinson"} Alexander Beilinson
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Lichtenbaum"} Stephen Lichtenbaum
            in the mid 1980s, and it was then defined by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
            in the mid 1990s.
          %p
            Motivic cohomology must not be confused with the hypothetical “universal” cohomology theory envisioned by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Alexander Grothendieck
            in the 1960s as the underlying reason for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+conjectures+on+algebraic+cycles"} standard conjectures on algebraic cycles
            %em absolute cohomology theory
            with values in abelian groups, while the latter is a
            %em geometric cohomology theory
            with values in the still conjectural abelian category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+motives"} mixed motives
            %em rational
            coefficents should appear as particular
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ext-group"} Ext-group
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/motive#DerivedMotives"} derived category of motives
          %p
            The motivic cohomology groups of a scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            form a bigraded family of abelian groups
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ,
                        %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^{p,q}(X,\mathbb{Z})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+scheme"} smooth
            over a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are the associated graded of the
            = succeed "-filtration" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            groups of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (at least if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+scheme"} regular
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+cohomology"} étale cohomology
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with coefficients in the sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_p
            of
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/roots+of+unity"} roots of unity
            (if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is invertible on
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            equals the characteristic of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The motivic cohomology of a sufficiently nice scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is also related to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            via the
            %em motivic spectral sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo ,
                      %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^{*,*}(X,\mathbb{Z}) \Rightarrow K_*(X).
          %p
            which degenerates rationally. The search for this spectral sequence was one of the motivating factor in the development of motivic cohomology. More generally, spectral sequences whose first page consists of motivic cohomology groups exist for any cohomology theory represented by a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+spectrum"} motivic spectrum
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Atiyah-Hirzebruch+spectral+sequence"} Atiyah-Hirzebruch spectral sequence
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            We give three definitions of motivic cohomology with integral coefficients, in historical order: the first, due to Bloch and later generalized by Levine, only works for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+schemes"} smooth schemes
            over
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+domain"} Dedekind domain
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+scheme"} smooth scheme
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
          %p
            Note that in each definition motivic cohomology is
            = succeed ":" do
              %em absolute
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^{p,q}(X,\mathbb{Z})
            depend only of the scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and not on any base scheme.
          %h3#HigherChowGroups As Bloch’s higher Chow groups
          %p
            The first and most elementary definition of motivic cohomology groups was Bloch’s definition of higher Chow groups (
            %a{:href => "#Bloch86"}> Bloch
            ), although they were only recognized as such later by Voevodsky.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+scheme"} smooth scheme
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z^*(X)
            of
            %em algebraic cycles
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the free abelian group generated by the irreducible closed subschemes of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The
            %strong
              algebraic
              = succeed "-simplex" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n
            is the
            = succeed "-scheme" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi i
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n = Spec(k[t_0, \dots, t_n]/(\sum_i t_i - 1)).
          %p
            Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n
            is isomorphic to affine
            = succeed "-space" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^\bullet
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosimplicial+object"} cosimplicial
            = succeed "-scheme." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+abelian+group"} simplicial abelian group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z^*(X,\bullet)
            is the subgroup of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z^*(X\times\Delta^\bullet)
            generated in simplicial degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            by the cycles which intersect all faces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi m
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times\Delta^m \subset X\times\Delta^n
            properly. One then defines the
            %strong higher Chow groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi CH
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH^*(X,n)
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi CH
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi z
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH^*(X,n) = \pi_n(z^*(X, \bullet)).
          %p
            The groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi CH
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CH^*(X,0)
            are the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+groups"} Chow groups
            of algebraic cycles modulo rational equivalence.
          %p
            Voevodsky proved that these groups agree with
            %a{:href => "#VoevodskysDef"} his definition
            of motivic cohomology under the re-indexing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi CH
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mi q
                  %mo −
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^{p,q}(X,\mathbb{Z}) = CH^{q}(X,2q-p) .
          %p
            Levine extended Bloch’s definition to smooth schemes over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+domains"} Dedekind domains
            in such a way that motivic cohomology supported at a prime fits in the expected long exact sequence (
            %a{:href => "#Levine01"}> Levine
            ).
          %h4#references References
          %ul#Bloch86
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spencer+Bloch"}> Spencer Bloch
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Algebraic Cycles and Higher
                  = succeed "-Theory" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi K
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %ul#Levine01
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Levine"}> Marc Levine
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Techniques of localization in the theory of algebraic cycles
              %a{:href => "http://www.uni-due.de/~bm0032/publ/TechLoc.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#VoevodskysDef As Zariski cohomology of certain complexes of sheaves
          %p
            In the mid 1990s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
            gave the first “official” definition of the motivic cohomology of a scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercohomology"} hypercohomology
            of certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexes"} complexes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}(q)
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+site"} Zariski
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (an analog of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
            of a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}(q)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q\geq 0
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong motivic complexes
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The motivic cohomology of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercohomology"} hypercohomology
              of the complexes of sheaves
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}(q)
              on the Zariski site:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo ,
                        %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mi Zar
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^{p,q}(X,\mathbb{Z}) := H^p_{Zar}(X,\mathbb{Z}(q))
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#MaVoWe"} MaVoWe, Definition 3.4
          %p
            Voevodsky’s definition, for smooth schemes over fields, has been shown to have most properties that Beilinson and Lichtenbaum had demanded of the hypothetical cohomology theory, except that to date it hasn’t been shown that the cohomology groups vanish in negative degree, as they should. This open question is known as the
            = succeed "." do
              %em Beilinson vanishing conjecture
          %h4#as_homsets_in_the_derived_category_of_motives As hom-sets in the derived category of motives
          %p
            Voevodsky also gave an accompanying definition of an integral version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of the hypothetical category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motive"} mixed motives
            (see there for the definition) and showed that the motivic cohomology appears as derived hom-complexes in this derived category (see
            %a{:href => "#MaVoWe"} MaVoWe, rop. 14.16
            for a precise statement).
          %h4#references_2 References
          %ul#MaVoWe
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlo+Mazza"}> Carlo Mazza
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Weibel"} Charles Weibel
              %em Lectures in motivic cohomology
              (
              %a{:href => "http://math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/motiviclectures.html"} web
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.claymath.org/library/monographs/cmim02.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#ChowGroupsAsInfStackCohomology As cohomology with coefficients in Eilenberg-Mac Lane objects
          %p
            From the point of view of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+homotopy+theory"} motivic homotopy theory
            of Morel and Voevodsky, one would like the motivic cohomology of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to be representable in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+homotopy+theory"} stable motivic homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SH(X)
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/motivic+homotopy+theory#CohomologyTheories"} bigraded cohomology theory
            represented by the
            %em motivic Eilenberg–Mac Lane spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi SH
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(\mathbb{Z})\in SH(X)
          %p
            The motivic spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(\mathbb{Z})
            is built out of
            %em motivic Eilenberg–Mac Lane spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z}(n),2n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            To define motivic Eilenberg-Mac Lane spaces, a first guess might be to apply the general definition of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Mac+Lane+object"} Eilenberg-Mac Lane object
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nisnevich+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} Nisnevich (∞,1)-topos
            and then take its
            = succeed "-localization." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
            = succeed "-spectrum" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            = succeed "-spectrum." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}^1
            %a{:href => "#ICM"} ICM-talk article
            (on p. 596) that every morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}^1 \wedge K(\mathbb{Z},n) \rightarrow K(\mathbb{Z},n+1)
            is trivial in the
            = succeed "-homotopy" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
          %p
            Instead one applies a recipe which, when applied to the usual topological spheres produces the (topological)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Mac+Lane+spaces"} Eilenberg-Mac Lane spaces
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}^1
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Thom+theorem"} Dold-Thom theorem
            says that in topology the reduced singular homology of a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            can be produced as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} colim
                    %mi N
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                    %mi N
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_i(X) = \pi_i (\mathrm{colim}_N\, \mathrm{Sym}^N X)^+
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                    %mi N
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Sym}^N X = (X\times X\times ...\times X)/\Sigma_N
            is the free strictly commutative monoid on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^+
            denotes group completion. Inserting the topological n-sphere
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
            yields that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                    %mi N
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathrm{Sym}^N X)^+
            is an Eilenberg-Mac Lane space.
          %p
            The symmetric powers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Sym}^N
            also make sense for quasi-projective
            = succeed "-schemes," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            on such schemes. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a pointed presheaf, we have maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Sym}^N (X) \rightarrow \mathrm{Sym}^{N+1} (X)
            (lengthening an
            = succeed "-letter" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(-):=(\mathrm{Sym}^{\infty} (-))^+
            %em motivic Eilenberg-Mac Lane spaces
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z}(n),2n):=L((\mathbb{P}^1)^{\wedge n}).
          %p
            These assemble to give the
            %em motivic Eilenberg-Mac Lane spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(\mathbb{Z}):=(\mathbb{Z},K(\mathbb{Z}(1),2),K(\mathbb{Z}(2),4),\ldots)
            with bonding maps induced by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℙ
                        %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℙ
                        %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}^1 \wedge \mathrm{Sym}^N ({\mathbb{P}^1}^{\wedge m}) \rightarrow \mathrm{Sym}^N ({\mathbb{P}^1}^{\wedge m+1}), (x, \sum x_i) \mapsto \sum (x,x_i)
            (i.e. take the extra
            = succeed "-point" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}^1
            = succeed "-product" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            = succeed "s)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}^1
          %p
            This definition does not quite work over fields of positive characteristic. In general one has to take cycles as described by Denis-Charles Cisinski below. Intuitively the points of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sym
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathrm{Sym}^{\infty} (X))^+
            are finite formal sums of points of X, i.e. zero-cycles, which links this story to the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            described below. In characteristic zero both coincide. The link to higher Chow groups however only becomes apparent in the cycle description.
          %blockquote
            %p
              the following paragraphs are due to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"} Denis-Charles Cisinski
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/2520/homotopy-theory-of-schemes-examples"} this MathOverflow thread
          %p
            To keep things simple, let us assume we work over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfect+field"} perfect field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Picard+group"} Picard group
            (i.e. the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+group"} Chow group
            in degree 1). This works essentially like in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} (model) category of simplicial Nisnevich sheaves
            (over smooth
            = succeed "-schemes)," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi m
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}_m := \mathbb{A}^1 - \{0\}
            has the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy type
            of the infinite dimensional projective space.
          %p
            Moreover, as the Picard group is homotopy invariant for regular schemes (semi-normal is even enough), the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Pic
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^1(X,\mathbb{G}_m) = Pic(X)
            reads as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi Pic
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi CH
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [X,\mathbf{B} \mathbb{G}_m]
                  \=
                  Pic(X)
                  \=
                  CH^1(X)
                  \\,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-]
            are the hom-sets in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+homotopy+category"} motivic homotopy category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{H}(k)
          %p
            In general, we denote by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z}(n),2n)
            the
            = succeed "-th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Mac+Lane+object"} Eilenberg-Mac Lane object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{H}(k)
            which represents the
            = succeed "-th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+group"} Chow group
            = succeed "-scheme" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi i
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\Sigma^i X , K(\mathbb{Z}(n), 2n)]
                  \=
                  H^{2n -i}(X, \mathbb{Z}(n))
                  \\,.
          %p
            There are several models for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z}(n),2n)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z}(1),2)
            is the infinite projective space.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z}(0),0)
            is simply the constant sheaf. For higher
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Given a
            = succeed "-scheme" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(X)
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+with+transfers"} presheaf with transfers
            associated to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-scheme" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            are the finite
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (i.e. the finite linear combinations of cycles
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum n_i Z_i
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \times X
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_i
            is finite and surjective over
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            makes that this is well defined without working up to rational equivalences, and as we consider only pullbacks along maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to V
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            smooth (hence regular) ensures that the multiplicities which will appear from these pullbacks will always be integers). The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(X)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nisnevich+topology"} Nisnevich topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (we will need this functoriality only for closed immersions).
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (resp.
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            (resp.
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi u
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u_i : Y \to X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Mac+Lane+object"} Eilenberg-Mac Lane object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\mathbb{Z}(n), 2n)
            is the sheaf of sets obtained as the quotient (in the category of Nisnevich sheaves of abelian groups) of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(X)
            by the subsheaf generated by the images of the maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi u
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(u_i) : L(Y) \to L(X)
          %h4#references_3 References
          %p The original definition of motivic Eilenberg–Mac Lane spaces and spectra is in
          %ul#ICM
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"}> Vladimir Voevodsky
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^1
                  \-homotopy Theory
              %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/DMJDMV/xvol-icm/00/Voevodsky.MAN.ps.gz"} ps
          %p More details are in §4 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Hoyois"}> Marc Hoyois
              ,
              %em From algebraic cobordism to motivic cohomology
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.mathematik.ur.de/hoyois/papers/hopkinsmorel.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %h2#applications Applications
          %p
            Motivic cohomology is used to construct examples of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+systems"} Euler systems
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+values+of+L-functions"} special values of L-functions
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#LoefflerZerbes18"} #LoefflerZerbes18
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
